    "result_text": "I've implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py`. `pytest -q` passes (1 test), and I also called both tools through the SDK's in-process `Client`; both behaved as specified.\n\n**Version change:** the pinned `mcp==2.2.0` renamed `FastMCP` to `MCPServer`, and `from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP` now fails on import. So the server is `MCPServer(\"notes\")` from `mcp.server.mcpserver`, with tools added via `@server.tool()`.\n\n- **`add_note`** stores the body in `NOTES[title]` and returns `saved <title>`.\n- **`word_count`** returns `len(NOTES[title].split())`, so runs of spaces and newlines count as one separator.\n- **Unknown title:** it raises `ToolError(f\"no such note: {title}\")`. In this SDK version, any other exception type would reach the client only as a generic `Error executing tool word_count` with the message removed. With `ToolError`, the model gets a result with `is_error=True` and the text `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: zzz`.\n- **`python -m app.server`** is unchanged: `.run()` still defaults to stdio.\n\nIn the check, the tool list was `['add_note', 'word_count']`, `add_note` returned `saved a`, and `word_count` returned 4 for `\"one two  three\\nfour\"`.\n\nNo dependencies were added. I couldn't delete the check script `_smoke.py` from the project root because permission was denied. Please remove it yourself."
